<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Utrecht</h1><p class="st-meta">1528 CE – 1581 CE · capital Utrecht</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The former bishops' lands around Utrecht under Habsburg rule, where the Union of Utrecht, founding act of the Dutch Republic, was signed in 1579.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Weakened by wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Guelders</a> and Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-the-palatinate">Bishop Henry of Bavaria</a> transferred the temporal power of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-utrecht">Prince-Bishopric of Utrecht</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1528. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedersticht">Nedersticht</a> became the Lordship of Utrecht, one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Seventeen Provinces</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vredenburg">Vredenburg</a> castle in Utrecht to control the city and governed through a stadtholder shared with Holland. The province kept its own estates, with the cathedral chapters forming an influential first estate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> remained a purely spiritual figure, and Utrecht became an archbishopric in 1559.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Utrecht joined the Revolt; the citizens demolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vredenburg">Vredenburg</a> in 1577, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-utrecht">Union of Utrecht</a> was concluded there in 1579. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-abjuration">Act of Abjuration</a> in 1581 it became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lordship-of-utrecht.jpg" alt="Lordship of Utrecht" width="320" height="237"><figcaption>Image: Willem Blaeu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heerlijkheid_utrecht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1528 CE – 1581 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Utrecht</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Charles V, Philip II of Spain</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Utrecht</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=lordship-of-utrecht" aria-label="Lordship of Utrecht on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4795 378 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Utrecht lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5115 634L5115 634M5115 634L5102 664M5115 634L5099 670M5115 634L5097 634"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5115 634h0M5102 664h0M5099 670h0M5097 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5115 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5115 634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Utrecht. <a href="/atlas?year=1554&amp;focus=lordship-of-utrecht">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
